---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import portrait from '../assets/about/human-in-nature.jpg';
---

<Base
  title="About | Michelle Chang"
  description="Product designer and systems-thinker in San Francisco building in supply chain, infrastructure, and sustainability."
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 pt-16 sm:px-8 sm:pt-24">
    <h1 class="text-display text-kelp-900">
      Born and bred in San Francisco.<br />Designing what's outside the window.
    </h1>

    <!-- Portrait sits beside the opening paragraphs rather than above them:
         on a page whose argument is "ecosystem-thinking," the photo is
         evidence, not decoration. It leads on mobile, where a side-by-side
         would collapse into an awkward stack anyway. -->
    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-14">
      <figure class="order-first lg:order-last lg:col-span-5">
        <div
          class="overflow-hidden rounded-[1.5rem_1.25rem_1.75rem_1.375rem] border border-sand-200 bg-white"
        >
          <!-- width is capped at 1000 deliberately. Without it Astro emits the
               full 3024px original as the src fallback — a 2.6MB file that
               no layout on this page ever needs. -->
          <Image
            src={portrait}
            alt="Michelle crouched on a mossy boulder at the base of a tall waterfall, looking up at the falling water in a basalt canyon lined with moss and fallen logs"
            width={1000}
            height={1333}
            widths={[420, 700, 1000]}
            sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 92vw, 26rem"
            loading="eager"
            class="w-full"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption class="font-ui mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-fog-500">
          Against the excellent TLC's better judgement, I still go chasing
          waterfalls. This one was in Washington State.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="prose-tide lg:col-span-7">
        <p>
          I grew up on LEGOs and Roald Dahl, feed on imagination, revel in ocean
          mist and Karl the fog, and run on matcha.
        </p>

        <p>
          Eight years in, my work spans privacy and security, agriculture,
          maritime shipping, climate tech, construction, and most recently
          applied AI. The domains look unrelated until you notice the shape they
          share: an expert user, disparate and ambiguous sources, and a
          consequential call to make before the data resolves.
        </p>

        <p>
          {/* Astro collapses the newline before an inline <a>, which glues it
              to the preceding word. {' '} forces a real space. The Spotify si=
              share-tracking parameter is stripped from the URL. */}
          I'm also the design founder of{' '}
          <a href="https://seafoamstudio.co" rel="noopener">Seafoam Studio</a>,
          a multidisciplinary creative studio exploring creativity through
          movement, storytelling, and community. And I host{' '}
          <a
            href="https://open.spotify.com/show/033Il0aozf3QpBOa6QDQXv"
            rel="noopener"><em>Sea People</em></a>, a podcast of stories about
          the sea and the people who steward it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-tide mt-4">
      <h2>Design philosophy</h2>

      <p>
        I'm motivated by social dynamics: what actually moves people and
        processes. That means leading with curiosity and using a service design
        and research lens to understand where people are coming from before
        deciding what to build.
      </p>

      <p>
        In practice: start with the people and teams involved in the decision,
        not the interface.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
